w: 1.~The wi- cked _ man's trans- _ gres- si- on with- in _ my heart thus says, Un- doub- _ ted- ly the fear _ of God is not _ be- fore his eyes.
w: 2.~Be- cause him- _ self he _ flat- te- reth in his _ own blin- ded eye, Un- til _ the ha- te- fulness _ be found of his _ in- iq- ui- ty.
w: 3.~Words from his _ mouth pro- _ cee- ding are, fraud and _ in- iq- ui- ty: He to _ be wise, and to _ do good, hath left _ off ut- ter- ly.
w: 4.~He mis- chief, _ ly- ing _ on his bed, most cun- _ ning- ly doth plot: He sets _ him- self in ways _ not good, ill he _ ab- hor- reth not.
w: 5.~Thy mer- cy, _ Lord, is _ in the heav'ns; thy truth _ doth reach the clouds: Thy jus- _ tice is like moun- _ tains great; thy judg- _ ments deep as floods:
w: 6.~Lord, thou pre- _ ser- vest _ man and beast. How pre- _ cious is thy grace! There- fore _ in sha- dow of _ thy wings men's sons _ their trust shall place.
w: 7.~They with the _ fat- ness _ of thy house shall be _ well sa- tis- fied; From ri- _ vers of thy plea- _ sures thou wilt drink _ to them pro- vide.
w: 8.~Be- cause of _ life the _ foun- tain pure re- mains _ a- lone with thee; And in _ that pur- est light _ of thine we clear- _ ly light shall see.
w: 9.~Thy lo- ving\-- _ kind- ness _ un- to them con- ti- _ nue that thee know; And still _ on men u- pright _ in heart thy right- _ eous- ness be- stow.
w: 10.~Let not the _ foot of _ cru- el pride come, and _ a- gainst me stand; And let _ me not re- mov- _ èd be, Lord, by _ the wick- ed's hand.
